\subsection{Related Work}

\subsubsection{Conversational Data Augmentation}

Dialogue data augmentation has long been a focal point in natural language processing. Early efforts concentrated on single-turn data expansion, covering semantic variants \cite{DBLP:conf/coling/ZhangZLZ13, zhang2014semantic}, paraphrase generation \cite{DBLP:conf/naacl/DongSZL15, li2016neural}, back-translation \cite{monteiri2017improving}, and data reordering \cite{li2018neural}. However, these techniques fail to address the core issue of low diversity in casual chat.

Recent studies have explored multi-turn data augmentation, such as utilizing reply-prediction models \cite{li2018neural}, back-translation \cite{li2018neural}, and retrieval-based methods \multiturn}. Our work differs by employing a multi-stage process to construct a diverse dataset. Unlike prior approaches, we leverage the intrinsic diversity of the reply-prediction model and a retrieval-based model to build a richer dataset through multi-turn stacking. This allows us to overcome the limitations of traditional single-turn augmentation and the constraints of existing multi-turn methods.

